# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the TSan CI job.
#
# Only third-party code that is not built with -fsanitize=thread may be
# listed here, each with a reason. Never suppress a report in Terra Soar's
# own code: fix it.
#
# Format: https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions
#
# Run with TSAN_OPTIONS=ignore_noninstrumented_modules=1 as well (CI does).
# Qt and glib are not built with TSan, so TSan can't see the locks of Qt's
# event queue (QMutex is a futex). QNetworkAccessManager runs HTTP on a
# thread of its own and hands every request, reply and header list to ours
# through that queue, so without the option each fetch is reported as a race
# between free, memcpy or realloc inside Qt and the other thread, with only
# Qt's innermost frame named: too little to suppress by name without hiding
# our own code. The option makes TSan ignore the memory those uninstrumented
# libraries touch themselves; accesses compiled into our code, including
# Qt's inline functions such as QByteArray::append, are still checked, so a
# race between two of our threads on a Qt container is still reported.

# Mesa's llvmpipe worker threads (software OpenGL, uninstrumented).
race:libLLVM
race:swrast_dri.so
race:libgallium

# GDAL's driver registration (GDALAllRegister, run once from
# ts::initialiseGdal() under std::call_once) takes its own CPL mutexes in
# an order TSan reports as a lock-order inversion. Both locks are internal to
# GDAL and taken on one thread; libgdal is not instrumented.
deadlock:libgdal.so

# Qt Test's watchdog thread (started in every Qt Test program):
# QWaitCondition::wakeAll() on the watchdog thread, then ~QWaitCondition()
# on the main thread after QThread::wait(). The join happens inside
# libQt6Core, which is not built with TSan, so TSan cannot see the ordering.
# Note: this also hides a real destroy-while-in-use bug with QWaitCondition
# in our own code, which the concurrency tests must catch another way.
race:QWaitCondition::~QWaitCondition
